      "text": "To find the final price of the shirt after both successive discounts, we can follow these steps:\n\n1. Calculate the price of the shirt after the first discount.\n2. Calculate the price of the shirt after the second discount on the already discounted price.\n3. Determine the single percent discount that would give the same final price as the two successive discounts.\n\nLet's start with the first step:\n\n1. **Calculate the price of the shirt after the first discount:**\n   The original price of the shirt is $30. The first discount is 20%, so the price after the first discount is:\n   \\[\n   30 \\times (1 - 0.20) = 30 \\times 0.80 = 24\n   \\]\n   So, the price after the first discount is $24.\n\n2. **Calculate the price of the shirt after the second discount:**\n   The second discount is 25% off the new price of $24. So, the price after the second discount is:\n   \\[\n   24 \\times (1 - 0.25) = 24 \\times 0.75 = 18\n   \\]\n   So, the price after the second discount is $18.\n\n3. **Determine the single percent discount that would give the same final price:**\n   We need to find the single percent discount that, when applied to the original price of $30, would result in a final price of $18. Let's denote this single discount by \\(x\\). The final price after the single discount is:\n   \\[\n   30 \\times (1 - \\frac{x}{100})\n   \\]\n   We want this to be equal to $18:\n   \\[\n   30 \\times \\left(1 - \\frac{x}{100}\\right) = 18\n   \\]\n   Solving for \\(x\\):\n   \\[\n   1 - \\frac{x}{100} = \\frac{18}{30} = 0.60\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\frac{x}{100} = 1 - 0.60 = 0.40\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   x = 0.40 \\times 100 = 40\n   \\]\n   So, the single percent discount that would give the same final price as the two successive discounts is \\(\\boxed{40}\\).",
