      "text": "To determine the single percent discount that would give the same final price as the two successive discounts, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. Calculate the sale price after the initial 20% discount.\n2. Calculate the price after the additional 25% discount on the sale price.\n3. Determine the equivalent single discount rate that would result in the same final price.\n\n**Step 1: Calculate the sale price after the initial 20% discount.**\n\nThe regular price of the shirt is $30. A 20% discount means the shirt is sold for 80% of its regular price.\n\\[\n\\text{Sale price} = 30 \\times (1 - 0.20) = 30 \\times 0.80 = 24\n\\]\nSo, the sale price after the initial discount is $24.\n\n**Step 2: Calculate the price after the additional 25% discount on the sale price.**\n\nNow, we apply a 25% discount to the sale price of $24.\n\\[\n\\text{Price after additional discount} = 24 \\times (1 - 0.25) = 24 \\times 0.75 = 18\n\\]\nSo, the final price after both discounts is $18.\n\n**Step 3: Determine the equivalent single discount rate.**\n\nLet the equivalent single discount rate be \\( x \\). This means that \\( (1 - x) \\) of the final price of $18 is the same as the original price of $30.\n\\[\n(1 - x) \\times 18 = 30\n\\]\nSolving for \\( x \\):\n\\[\n1 - x = \\frac{30}{18} = \\frac{5}{3}\n\\]\n\\[\nx = 1 - \\frac{5}{3} = \\frac{3}{3} - \\frac{5}{3} = -\\frac{2}{3}\n\\]\nThe equivalent single discount rate is \\(\\frac{2}{3}\\), which is approximately 66.67%.\n\nTherefore, the single percent discount that would give the same final price as the two successive discounts is \\(\\boxed{66.67}\\).",
