    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To determine the final price of the shirt after applying the successive discounts and then finding the equivalent single percent discount, we will follow these steps:\n\n1. **Calculate the price after the first discount:**\n   The first discount is 20% off the regular price of $30.\n   \\[\n   \\text{First discount amount} = 30 \\times \\frac{20}{100} = 30 \\times 0.20 = 6\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\text{Price after first discount} = 30 - 6 = 24\n   \\]\n\n2. **Calculate the price after the second discount:**\n   The second discount is 25% off the new price of $24.\n   \\[\n   \\text{Second discount amount} = 24 \\times \\frac{25}{100} = 24 \\times 0.25 = 6\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\text{Final price} = 24 - 6 = 18\n   \\]\n\n3. **Determine the equivalent single percent discount:**\n   The final price of $18 is obtained by applying an initial discount of 20% followed by a second discount of 25%. Let \\( x \\) be the single percent discount that would achieve the same final price.\n\n   The price after the first discount is \\( 30 \\times (1 - \\frac{x}{100}) \\).\n   The price after the second discount is \\( 30 \\times (1 - \\frac{x}{100}) \\times (1 - \\frac{25}{100}) \\).\n\n   We set up the equation:\n   \\[\n   30 \\times \\left(1 - \\frac{x}{100}\\right) \\times \\left(1 - \\frac{25}{100}\\right) = 18\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   30 \\times \\left(1 - \\frac{x}{100}\\right) \\times \\frac{3}{4} = 18\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   30 \\times \\frac{3}{4} \\times \\left(1 - \\frac{x}{100}\\right) = 18\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   22.5 \\times \\left(1 - \\frac{x}{100}\\right) = 18\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   1 - \\frac{x}{100} = \\frac{18}{22.5}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   1 - \\frac{x}{100} = 0.8\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\frac{x}{100} = 0.2\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   x = 0.2 \\times 100\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   x = 20\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the single percent discount that would give the same final price as the two successive discounts is \\(\\boxed{20}\\).",
